  "briefing": "Turn 136. You are the Mongols: five cities, sixteen soldiers (four Crossbowmen, an Arquebusier, three Composite Archers, seven Phalangites and a Cataphract), 24 Gold, and a war with Switzerland that has stood since turn 78 without a battle for fifty turns. Switzerland's Aarau, a walled city, sits six hexes from your Khanbaliq. The Swiss army is one Crossbowman and two Scouts, and the Swiss treasury holds 500 Gold. In the real game the Mongol AI (Hard) began a siege on turn 139 and took Aarau on turn 147. Take Aarau by turn 149.",
  "tests": "The war the AI has already declared and parked: waiting for a siege it can forecast, then massing every shooter on a walled city at once from two hexes, keeping the melee back until the city and its garrison are shot down, replacing losses from the line, and walking a Phalangite into the empty city. This variant gives Switzerland 500 Gold instead of the 2,235 the human player left unspent: since territory sight and the attack reveal (2026-10-18) a Hard Swiss defender sees the army coming and buys six garrison units with that much Gold (The Golden Horde comes then reads 0%), while with 700 Gold or less the attack takes Aarau every time. It is the attack regression check for the attacker's march and siege.",
